<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milan I of Serbia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Milan Obrenović</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> Obrenović ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> as prince from 1868 and as its first modern king from 1882 to 1889. Under him Serbia fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1876–1878 and won full independence at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a>. Closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, he was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1885 and abdicated in 1889 in favour of his son Alexander.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milan-i-of-serbia.jpg" alt="Milan I of Serbia" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josef Löwy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exk%C3%B6nig_Milan_(Wiener_Bilder_1901).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
